---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

// The ten rubric dimensions, transcribed from ai-radar's evals/rubric.md. `computed: true`
// marks the two that never reach a model — they are the point of the piece, so they carry a
// visual distinction rather than a footnote.
const quality = [
  { dim: 'A1', name: 'signal_density', measures: 'Non-obvious insight per paragraph; no filler.', anchor: '5 = every line earns its space. 1 = restated abstracts.' },
  { dim: 'A2', name: 'source_integrity', measures: 'Primary sources over aggregators; no broken links.', anchor: 'Capped at 2 by any broken URL. 5 = every claim links to a primary source.', computed: true },
  { dim: 'A3', name: 'focus_alignment', measures: 'Fits my topics without becoming an echo chamber.', anchor: '5 = strong fit plus one adjacent surprise. 1 = drift.' },
  { dim: 'A4', name: 'delta_clarity', measures: '"What changed" is a real diff, not a restatement.', anchor: '5 = explicit new/climbing/cooled with reasons. 1 = the same items relisted.' },
  { dim: 'A5', name: 'coverage', measures: 'Inverse of important stories it missed.', anchor: '0 missed = 5. 4 or more = 1.' },
];

const experience = [
  { dim: 'X1', name: 'board_legibility', measures: 'A cold reader finds the top story in under ten seconds.', anchor: '5 = the top row is unambiguously the story of the day.' },
  { dim: 'X2', name: 'instrument_honesty', measures: 'Machine opinion is labelled as such; observed signals dominate.', anchor: '5 = attribution and real numbers dominate. 1 = a brief presented as fact.' },
  { dim: 'X3', name: 'freshness', measures: 'Hours between publication and grading.', anchor: 'Under 6h = 5. 6–12 = 4. 12–24 = 3. 24–36 = 2. Over 36 = 1.', computed: true },
  { dim: 'X4', name: 'failure_surface', measures: 'One dead feed does not take the digest with it.', anchor: '5 = isolated failures, quiet-window fallback works. 1 = one failure cascades.' },
  { dim: 'X5', name: 'coupling', measures: 'Collect, distill and render stay independently regenerable.', anchor: '5 = strict boundaries. 1 = imports cross them.' },
];

const REPO = 'https://github.com/amaljithkuttamath/ai-radar';
---

<Base
  title="How the radar grades itself"
  description="A daily AI newsletter that scores its own output against a fixed rubric — and the three rules that decide whether that score means anything."
>
  <section class="page-header animate-in">
    <h1>How the radar grades itself</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">
      Every morning something reads yesterday's digest and gives it ten scores out of five.
      Here is why that number is worth anything — and the month it wasn't.
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="prose animate-in animate-in-delay-1">
    <p>
      <a href="/radar">The radar</a> publishes a digest of AI research and releases every day.
      It is assembled by a model, which means the interesting question is not whether it runs
      but whether it is any good — and "is it good" is exactly the question the thing producing
      it cannot answer about itself.
    </p>

    <p>
      So a second process grades the first. It reads the day's digest, checks every link,
      scores ten dimensions against a fixed rubric, and commits the result as JSON alongside
      the digest it judged. Over time those files are a trend line, and a trend line is the
      only thing that distinguishes "today was thin" from "this has been getting worse for
      three weeks."
    </p>

    <p>
      Getting a model to emit ten numbers is trivial. Getting ten numbers that mean something
      is the whole problem, and it comes down to three rules.
    </p>

    <h2>Rule one: the grader must not share a family with the writer</h2>

    <p>
      A model evaluating output from its own family self-enhances — it scores that output
      roughly 10–25% higher than a neutral judge would. Point a model at its own work and it
      will tell you the work is good.
    </p>

    <p>
      What makes this dangerous is not the inflation. It is that the failure is
      <em>invisible by construction</em>. The scores stay green, the trend line stays flat and
      healthy, and the digest quietly degrades underneath. The instrument and the subject agree,
      so there is no disagreement anywhere for you to notice. You would find out from a reader,
      months late, or never.
    </p>

    <p>
      The rule was written down early. It sat in the contract, in bold, as a paragraph a
      language model was asked to honour. That is not a control — it is a suggestion with good
      formatting. It is now a fence: the grader resolves its own model family and the writer's,
      compares them, and refuses to run if they match. It also refuses a model id it does not
      recognise, rather than assuming an unfamiliar name is safe. A gate that opens when it is
      confused is not a gate, and a new provider appearing is precisely the moment a stale
      allowlist would wave a violation through.
    </p>

    <h2>Rule two: anything checkable gets checked, not judged</h2>

    <p>
      Two of the ten dimensions never reach the model at all.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Freshness (X3)</strong> is arithmetic. It is the number of hours between the
      digest's publication and the moment it was graded, mapped through fixed anchors. There is
      no judgement in it, so no model gets to be generous about it. The publication time comes
      from the git commit, not from any date inside the file — a pipeline that dies mid-write,
      or writes a stale-but-well-formed digest, leaves its own header looking perfectly current.
      Git's clock is the one clock the pipeline does not author.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Source integrity (A2)</strong> has a hard ceiling. Every link in the digest is
      requested before the model sees anything; if one is genuinely dead, A2 is capped at 2
      regardless of what the judge thought of the sourcing. A model cannot observe an HTTP
      status. Ask it to, and it will produce a plausible one. So the one quality dimension with
      a falsifiable answer is decided by the answer, not by a reading of it.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a nuance worth stating, because it is the difference between a signal and noise:
      a link that is <em>unreachable from the grading machine</em> does not cap anything. A
      proxy, a network policy, a local outage — those are facts about the grader, not the
      digest, and punishing yesterday's writing for today's connectivity puts pure noise into a
      trend line you are meant to read over months.
    </p>

    <div class="rubric animate-in animate-in-delay-2">
      <div class="rubric-axis">
        <h3 class="axis-head">Axis A — answer quality</h3>
        <p class="axis-sub">The digest itself.</p>
        {quality.map((d) => (
          <div class={`dim${d.computed ? ' dim-computed' : ''}`}>
            <div class="dim-head">
              <span class="mono dim-id">{d.dim}</span>
              <span class="mono dim-name">{d.name}</span>
              {d.computed && <span class="dim-badge">not judged</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="dim-measures">{d.measures}</p>
            <p class="dim-anchor">{d.anchor}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="rubric-axis">
        <h3 class="axis-head">Axis X — experience &amp; architecture</h3>
        <p class="axis-sub">How it reaches the reader.</p>
        {experience.map((d) => (
          <div class={`dim${d.computed ? ' dim-computed' : ''}`}>
            <div class="dim-head">
              <span class="mono dim-id">{d.dim}</span>
              <span class="mono dim-name">{d.name}</span>
              {d.computed && <span class="dim-badge">not judged</span>}
            </div>
            <p class="dim-measures">{d.measures}</p>
            <p class="dim-anchor">{d.anchor}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="rubric-note">
      Each axis averages to an overall; the two averages average again. Every score carries a
      one-sentence justification that must cite a specific item, URL or line. A score without
      evidence is treated as a bug, not a score — the number is not the artefact, the paper
      trail is.
    </p>

    <h2>Rule three: the critic runs outside the thing it criticises</h2>

    <p>
      The grader does not run in the pipeline's CI. If it did, a bug bad enough to break the
      digest could take its critic down in the same breath — and the failure mode that actually
      worries me is subtler: a change that degrades the output <em>and</em> suppresses the
      complaint about it. A critic sharing a runtime with its subject can be silenced by its
      subject.
    </p>

    <p>
      So it runs on its own schedule, with its own credential, on a model from a different
      family, triggered from somewhere else entirely. Time-based, not chained to the pipeline:
      if the digest never appears, that produces a bad freshness score or an escalation, which
      is exactly the signal you want. A grader that only runs when the pipeline succeeds can
      never tell you the pipeline stopped.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does with a bad score</h2>

    <p>
      Scores that nobody acts on are decoration. Three conditions open a GitHub issue: any
      dimension at 2 or below, any genuinely broken URL, or the same dimension sitting at 3 or
      below for three consecutive days. That last one matters most — it catches the slow
      decline that no single day would flag, which is the failure a daily eye is worst at
      seeing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Every run also appends an improvement item tied to the lowest-scoring dimension of the
      day. Tied to it specifically: the item names the dimension and its score, so it cannot
      drift into a generic wishlist entry. A backlog of "make it better" is a backlog nobody
      works.
    </p>

    <h2>The month it did not run</h2>

    <p>
      On the 13th of July, the grader stopped. I found out twenty-six days later.
    </p>

    <p>
      Nothing failed. That is the entire lesson. A scheduled task that silently stops produces
      no error, no log line, and no alert — from the outside, "ran fine" and "never ran" are the
      same observation. And because the grader lived outside the repository by design, it
      appeared in no workflow list, no run history, and no file. There was nothing anywhere that
      could notice its absence.
    </p>

    <p>
      Worse, the pipeline was healthy throughout. Digests published every morning, on time, all
      through the outage. Every surface I would have glanced at was green. The thing that was
      broken was the only thing that could have told me anything was wrong.
    </p>

    <p>
      Two changes came out of that. The first is a watchdog that checks the <em>artifact</em>
      rather than the process — how old is the newest eval file? Staleness is the one signal
      that stays true regardless of why something stopped: task deleted, token expired, silent
      error, provider outage. It does not need to know the cause to know there is one.
    </p>

    <p>
      The second is that the grader now has an implementation in the repository instead of
      existing only as a prompt inside a scheduled task. The specification was always there —
      two hundred lines of it, every rule written as a warning because something had got it
      wrong once. But a specification with no implementation has no tests, no version history,
      and no runs to be absent from. The execution still happens outside, which was the correct
      decision and is unchanged. What moved inside is the part that could be looked at.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <p class="closing">
      The uncomfortable version of all this: I built a system to tell me whether my automated
      newsletter was any good, and then did not notice for most of a month that it had stopped
      telling me. The rubric was fine. The dimensions were fine. What was missing was any way to
      distinguish a good report from no report — which, it turns out, is the same class of
      problem the rubric exists to solve, one level up.
    </p>

    <p class="source-note">
      Everything here is in <a href={REPO}>amaljithkuttamath/ai-radar</a> — the
      <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/evals/rubric.md`}>rubric</a>,
      the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/grader`}>grader</a>,
      the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/docs/architecture/adr`}>decision records</a>,
      and every eval it has ever written.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .rubric {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    margin: var(--space-xl) 0;
  }

  @media (min-width: 720px) {
    .rubric { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
  }

  .rubric-axis {
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    border: 1px solid var(--fg-faint);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    padding: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .axis-head {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-subsection);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin: 0;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  }

  .axis-sub {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin: 0.15rem 0 var(--space-md);
  }

  .dim {
    padding: var(--space-sm) 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--fg-faint);
  }

  /* The two computed dimensions are the argument of the piece, so they are marked in the
     layout rather than explained in a caption. A left rule reads as "this one is different"
     without needing a legend. */
  .dim-computed {
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent-color);
    padding-left: var(--space-md);
    margin-left: calc(var(--space-md) * -1 + 2px);
  }

  .dim-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .dim-id {
    font-size: var(--text-label);
    color: var(--accent-color);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .dim-name {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .dim-badge {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-tag);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--fg-faint);
    border-radius: var(--radius-full);
    padding: 0.05rem 0.45rem;
    margin-left: auto;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .dim-measures {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    margin: 0.3rem 0 0.15rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .dim-anchor {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-tag);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .prose .rubric-note {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .prose .closing {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--text-body-lg);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .prose .source-note {
    font-size: var(--text-small);
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>
